  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts`, and it passes `typecheck`. I could not run `vite build` or view a rendered frame, so none of the visuals or light intensities have been checked.\n\n- **Car:** the body, a raked black-glass cabin, a chrome ring around the cabin, two chrome side strips, four rubber wheels and chrome hubs are all generated in code from parametric meshes.\n- **Paint:** the body uses a deep candy-red `material.physical` with metallic 0.55, clearcoat 1 and a procedurally generated flake normal map. A custom additive shader shell sits 4 mm above the body and cabin. It reflects an analytic studio with five long strip softboxes (one overhead, four vertical), weighted by Fresnel at the clearcoat's index of refraction. On the paint shell, random flake cells add glints.\n- **Turntable:** the platform is a dark, partly transparent glossy deck with an emissive cool-white ring light at its rim and a thin ring on top. A vertically mirrored copy of the car sits below the deck, so the reflection shows faintly through it.\n- **Showroom:** the background is near-black. There are three overhead and side rect-light strips, a front fill strip and a cool rim light. Two glowing strip fixtures stand behind the car, plus a low-intensity environment and mild bloom.\n- **Camera and state:** the camera is fixed, low and front three-quarter. The `turntable` group rotates at 20°/s based on `performance.now()`. `window.__TASK_STATE__` is updated every frame with the unwrapped angle in degrees and `paintFinish: 'clearcoat'`. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()` and one more animation frame.\n\nTreat these as the likely failure points:\n- A GLSL compile error in the coat shell is skipped silently, which would leave the car without its strip reflections.\n- Light and bloom intensities were chosen without a capture, so the frame may be too bright or too dim.\n- The faint mirror under the deck depends on the transparent-deck blending behaving as expected.",
